---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/water-break-test-contact-angle-particle-cleanliness';
const title = 'Water-Break Test vs Contact Angle: What About Particle Cleanliness?';
const seoTitle = 'Water-Break Test vs Contact Angle: Particle Cleanliness';
const description = 'Interpret water-break, contact-angle and particle-inspection reports without confusing their results. Match each observation to the cleaning requirement.';
const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Does a water-break pass mean there are no particles?',
    a: 'No. A wetting observation does not provide a particle count or size distribution. Keep the particle inspection result separate.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a contact-angle change identify the contamination?',
    a: 'No. The angle alone does not identify the chemical substance responsible for a difference; that needs other analytical evidence.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Which result should support a dry particle-cleaning review?',
    a: 'Use particle evidence suited to the material and agreed requirement. Keep its inspection method, area and reporting limits attached to the result.'
  }
];
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/wetting-particle-evidence-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/wetting-particle-evidence-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-10-20', dateModified: '2026-10-20', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqItems.map(item => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a } }))
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Wetting and Particle Evidence</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Surface Cleaning</span><span class="blog-tag">Report Reading</span><span class="blog-tag">Wettability</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">A cleaning review may include a water-break result, a contact angle and a particle inspection report. They can all be useful, but they do not answer the same question. Read the measured property before deciding whether a result addresses the contamination you wanted to remove.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>October 20, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>4 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/wetting-particle-evidence-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/wetting-particle-evidence-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/wetting-particle-evidence-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/wetting-particle-evidence-v1.jpg" alt="Conceptual comparison of water-break, contact-angle and particle-inspection records, each paired with its own reporting context. No test values." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original conceptual illustration. Three kinds of evidence; keep each result with its method. No test values.</em></p>

      <section id="water-break">
        <h2>Water-break testing concerns a wetting observation</h2>
        <p>The public description of <a href="https://store.astm.org/f0022-21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM F22-21</a> concerns detecting hydrophobic surface films. It describes a qualitative assessment, commonly used for a pass/fail decision. This is a different output from counting particles.</p>
        <p>Before interpreting a reported pass, establish which material face, method and acceptance requirement it refers to. Do not assume a procedure used on a metal part is suitable for a coated film or assembled board. This article interprets records; it is not an instruction to wet your product.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="contact-angle">
        <h2>A contact angle needs its measurement context</h2>
        <p>The public scope of <a href="https://store.astm.org/d7334-08r22.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D7334-08(2022)</a> covers <strong>advancing</strong> contact-angle measurements. The reported angle concerns wetting by the test liquid; it does not directly provide a surface-tension value. The public description also distinguishes an observed difference from identifying the chemical groups responsible, which requires other analysis.</p>
        <p>Keep the liquid and angle type with the result. A smaller number is not, by itself, a particle-removal percentage or a universal release criterion.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="separate-evidence">
        <h2>Keep particle and residue requirements separate</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://www.iest.org/Standards-RPs/Recommended-Practices/IEST-STD-CC1246" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IEST-STD-CC1246E overview</a> treats particles and molecular residue within product-cleanliness requirements. It places those requirements in the context of the application and agreed limits, rather than imposing one requirement on every product.</p>
        <p>Our interpretation of these different measurement scopes is straightforward: a wetting result does not directly supply particle numbers or sizes. Equally, a lower particle count does not establish a particular wetting condition or chemical-residue result. Avoid renaming either observation as a general “cleanliness score.”</p>
      </section>

      <section id="report-comparison">
        <h2>Read the result without adding a missing conclusion</h2>
        <p>These are hypothetical report-reading examples, not factory results or a prescribed test plan.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Wetting and particle evidence comparison">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">A report says</th><th scope="col">Keep with that result</th><th scope="col">Do not infer</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Water-break assessment passed.</th><td>The applicable method, material face and decision rule.</td><td>Zero particles across the product.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Contact angle changed.</th><td>The test liquid, angle type and compared surface locations.</td><td>The identity or quantity of contamination removed.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Fewer particles were reported.</th><td>The inspection method, area, size definition and reporting limit.</td><td>That a wetting, chemical-residue or bonding requirement also passed.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>For the counting details, see <a href="/blog/surface-particle-inspection-report">reading a surface particle inspection report</a>. Keeping that evidence separate avoids replacing a specific result with a broader claim it did not test.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="review-questions">
        <h2>Use three questions in a cleaning review</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>What was the required outcome?</strong> Copy the requirement and its document reference into the review. If it is missing, ask the responsible process owner.</li>
          <li><strong>Which record actually addresses it?</strong> Attach the report, sample identity and method. Mark unanswered requirements as unresolved rather than borrowing a pass from another test.</li>
          <li><strong>What else is needed for the decision?</strong> Assign an owner to missing evidence and record the decision basis. Do not treat an incomplete record as an equipment-performance claim.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>These are proposed review questions, not a standard procedure. Preserve material identity using the <a href="/blog/coated-optical-film-cleaning-trial-samples">coated-film sample preparation guide</a>. If the task involves flux or other residue removal, first clarify the process boundary in <a href="/blog/dry-pcb-cleaning-vs-flux-removal">dry PCB cleaning versus flux removal</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="faq"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq-list">{faqItems.map(item => <details class="faq-item"><summary>{item.q}</summary><p>{item.a}</p></details>)}</div></section>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Match the cleaning discussion to your requirement</h2>
        <p>Share the material, process stage, contamination concern and available inspection records. Our team can discuss the dry particle-cleaning application and the information needed for an equipment review.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/cleaning-machine">Explore Surface Cleaning Equipment &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Wetting%20and%20Particle%20Evidence%20Review">Discuss your cleaning evidence &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
